  "limitations": [
    "A consistent 'frontal tendency' in judged slant, where perceived slants were biased towards a frontal surface, was observed. This was attributed to factors such as the visibility of the projection-screen's texture, the absence of differential blur and accommodation cues, the tendency to conform to the slant of the hole-screen, and any residual head-motion (p. 16).",
    "The projection screen in the substantiation experiment had a more visible texture, and differences in set-up contributed to lesser slopes in the psychophysical correspondence (p. 17-18)."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This empirical study investigated the perception of visual surfaces, specifically how the gradient of texture-density in a projected image influences the perceived optical slant of a surface. Using a controlled monocular viewing setup, the experiment demonstrated a psychophysical correspondence: as the gradient of texture-density increased, the perceived slant of the surface also increased. The direction of the gradient determined the direction of the perceived slant (e.g., upward density increase yielded a floorwise slant). Regular textures produced a clearer and more definite impression of slant compared to irregular textures. A key limitation was a consistent 'frontal tendency' in judgments, where perceived slants were biased towards a frontal surface, possibly due to conflicting cues from the experimental setup like the visible texture of the projection screen or the absence of differential blur.",
